A||||3||Adrift||Brake Caliper Rebuild Question (Pic Inside)||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-12-2001||04:54 PM||||I bought a rebuilt brake caliper and need to transfer some parts from my old caliper to my new one - the only problem is I can't figure out how to get the parts off the old one! I got the rubber off no problem, but couldn't get the screw things the red arrows are pointing at out! Any help?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP_1648[1].jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-12-2001||06:58 PM||||If you can't just push them out then they are probably trash.<P>Just buy a new set of hardware...<P>that will give you:<BR>new slide bolts with new O rings and boots.<BR>new pad retainer clip for inner pad.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Adrift||12-12-2001||07:50 PM||||You can buy these at your local NAPA?||||reg||1
Z||000003||honegod||12-12-2001||08:08 PM||||it sounds likely that the pins are stuck to the Orings in the caliper bores.<P>the pins can move in both directions, so it would be easy to clamp the caliper in a vise and, using a chunk of wood, hammer the pin in, breaking the Orings loose.<P>rotating the pins while pushing them in and out helps them slide a LOT easier.<P>if the pins are just grungy, those nonabrasive 3M green scouring pads clean them up nicely, if there is rust on the sliding surface of the pin, replace it.<p>[This message has been edited by honegod (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
